<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Köthen</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cöthen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Köthen is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a>. From 1603 to 1847 it was the residence of the princes, later dukes, of Anhalt-Köthen. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-sebastian-bach">Johann Sebastian Bach</a> worked at its court from 1717 to 1723, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fruitbearing-society">Fruitbearing Society</a> for the German language had its seat there. The founder of homeopathy, Samuel Hahnemann, also lived and practised in the town.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kothen.jpg" alt="Köthen" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MEH Bergmann · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:K%C3%B6then_in_Anhalt_Silhouette_Rathaus_Schloss_Rapsfeld_Aufnahme_und_Copyright_MEH_Bergmann.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
